1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,475
A few minutes before two o'clock on the

2
00:00:02,495 --> 00:00:05,010
morning of August fifth, 1944,

3
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:07,944
it was freezing in the hills of New South

4
00:00:07,964 --> 00:00:08,921
Wales, in Australia.

5
00:00:08,941 --> 00:00:11,529
Inside a prisoner of war camp outside the

6
00:00:11,549 --> 00:00:13,811
country town of Cowra, more than a

7
00:00:13,831 --> 00:00:15,766
thousand Japanese prisoners lay awake in

8
00:00:15,786 --> 00:00:17,090
their huts, fully dressed.

9
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:22,122
Then a man ran out of the dark toward the

10
00:00:22,142 --> 00:00:23,955
gate of the Japanese compound,

11
00:00:23,975 --> 00:00:25,422
shouting at the sentries.

12
00:00:25,442 --> 00:00:27,988
Nobody outside the wire understood what he

13
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:28,722
was shouting.

14
00:00:28,742 --> 00:00:31,655
A guard fired his rifle into the air.

15
00:00:31,675 --> 00:00:33,488
And somewhere in the compound,

16
00:00:33,508 --> 00:00:34,975
a single bugle sounded.

17
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:40,033
Two guards came out of their hut in the dark.

18
00:00:40,053 --> 00:00:42,286
Ben Hardy was forty-five years old.

19
00:00:42,306 --> 00:00:43,787
Ralph Jones was forty-three.

20
00:00:43,807 --> 00:00:46,414
They pulled their army greatcoats on over

21
00:00:46,434 --> 00:00:49,417
their pyjamas and ran for a machine gun

22
00:00:49,437 --> 00:00:52,440
that stood on a trailer beside the wire.

23
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:56,172
Behind them, hundreds of men were already

24
00:00:56,192 --> 00:00:57,567
coming over the fence.

25
00:00:57,587 --> 00:01:00,008
They carried knives from the mess hall,

26
00:01:00,028 --> 00:01:02,470
clubs studded with nails, and baseball bats.

27
00:01:03,775 --> 00:01:06,180
This is the documented story of the Cowra

28
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:06,483
breakout:

29
00:01:06,503 --> 00:01:08,606
the largest prison break of the Second

30
00:01:08,626 --> 00:01:10,424
World War, told from the Australian

31
00:01:10,444 --> 00:01:12,547
official history, from the findings of the

32
00:01:12,567 --> 00:01:13,759
military court of inquiry,

33
00:01:13,779 --> 00:01:16,184
from the citation that gave two guards the

34
00:01:16,204 --> 00:01:18,307
George Cross, and from the recorded words

35
00:01:18,327 --> 00:01:20,126
of the men who were there,

36
00:01:20,146 --> 00:01:21,055
guards and prisoners.

37
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:24,600
The man who blew that bugle had been

38
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:26,500
living at Cowra for nineteen months under

39
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,127
a name that was not his.

40
00:01:28,147 --> 00:01:30,569
To understand why, we have to go back two

41
00:01:30,589 --> 00:01:33,282
and a half years, to a beach on an island

42
00:01:33,302 --> 00:01:34,930
off the north coast of Australia.

43
00:01:36,775 --> 00:01:40,299
In January 1941, the Japanese Army issued

44
00:01:40,319 --> 00:01:42,896
a code of conduct for every soldier,

45
00:01:42,916 --> 00:01:44,380
the Field Service Code.

46
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,606
One line in it became famous.

47
00:01:46,626 --> 00:01:48,461
In the government's own English

48
00:01:48,481 --> 00:01:49,574
translation, it reads:

49
00:01:49,594 --> 00:01:52,542
"If alive, do not suffer the disgrace of

50
00:01:52,562 --> 00:01:53,675
becoming a prisoner."

51
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:57,229
The code belonged to the army,

52
00:01:57,249 --> 00:02:00,528
but the idea ran through the whole of the

53
00:02:00,548 --> 00:02:01,261
Japanese forces.

54
00:02:01,281 --> 00:02:04,193
A soldier who was captured had not just

55
00:02:04,213 --> 00:02:05,293
lost a fight.

56
00:02:05,313 --> 00:02:08,592
He had shamed his name, his family and his

57
00:02:08,612 --> 00:02:08,958
village.

58
00:02:08,978 --> 00:02:10,791
Many Japanese prisoners preferred that

59
00:02:10,811 --> 00:02:13,010
their families believe they were dead.

60
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,909
So Japanese prisoners were rare.

61
00:02:16,929 --> 00:02:20,521
By August 1944, Australia held two

62
00:02:20,541 --> 00:02:22,642
thousand two hundred and twenty-three of

63
00:02:22,662 --> 00:02:25,116
them, and five hundred and forty-four of

64
00:02:25,136 --> 00:02:26,531
those were merchant seamen,

65
00:02:26,551 --> 00:02:27,238
not soldiers.

66
00:02:27,258 --> 00:02:30,066
In the same camps there were more than

67
00:02:30,086 --> 00:02:31,500
fourteen thousand Italian prisoners.

68
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:35,355
The Australian official history set down

69
00:02:35,375 --> 00:02:36,618
the contrast plainly.

70
00:02:36,638 --> 00:02:39,144
Most of the twenty-one thousand Australian

71
00:02:39,164 --> 00:02:41,669
prisoners in Japanese hands were under-fed

72
00:02:41,689 --> 00:02:44,195
and over-worked, and many were dying.

73
00:02:44,215 --> 00:02:46,299
The Japanese prisoners in Australian

74
00:02:46,319 --> 00:02:48,825
camps, it says, were well fed,

75
00:02:48,845 --> 00:02:50,508
living in comfortable quarters,

76
00:02:50,528 --> 00:02:51,370
and thriving.

77
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:54,990
On the morning of February nineteenth,

78
00:02:55,010 --> 00:02:58,458
1942, Japanese carrier planes and bombers

79
00:02:58,478 --> 00:03:00,286
attacked the port of Darwin,

80
00:03:00,306 --> 00:03:01,749
in Australia's far north.

81
00:03:01,769 --> 00:03:04,674
It was the first air raid on Darwin.

82
00:03:04,694 --> 00:03:06,502
Bombs fell on the harbour,

83
00:03:06,522 --> 00:03:08,350
the town and the airfield.

84
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:11,956
One of the fighter pilots that morning was

85
00:03:11,976 --> 00:03:15,770
a young naval airman named Hajime Toyoshima.

86
00:03:15,790 --> 00:03:19,650
He flew a Zero from the aircraft carrier Hiryu.

87
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:21,618
Over Darwin his plane was hit.

88
00:03:21,638 --> 00:03:23,586
With his engine losing oil pressure,

89
00:03:23,606 --> 00:03:26,230
he could not get back to the carrier.

90
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:29,400
He came down on Melville Island,

91
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:32,066
north of Darwin, home of the Tiwi people.

92
00:03:32,086 --> 00:03:34,561
The crash landing threw him forward into

93
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:37,055
his own gun breeches and his instrument

94
00:03:37,075 --> 00:03:39,570
panel, and his face was badly cut.

95
00:03:40,875 --> 00:03:42,892
He wandered through the bush.

96
00:03:42,912 --> 00:03:44,678
According to the Tiwi account,

97
00:03:44,698 --> 00:03:47,495
he walked into a camp of Tiwi women and

98
00:03:47,515 --> 00:03:48,975
picked up a baby.

99
00:03:48,995 --> 00:03:51,975
Not far away, a Tiwi hunting party was out.

100
00:03:53,575 --> 00:03:55,962
The man who caught him was a Tiwi man

101
00:03:55,982 --> 00:03:58,835
named Matthias Ulungura.

102
00:03:58,855 --> 00:04:00,808
Years later, his account was written down

103
00:04:00,828 --> 00:04:01,955
in his own words.

104
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,720
"I see him through the bushes and I go

105
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:06,567
first," he said.

106
00:04:06,587 --> 00:04:09,389
"I told the other men to run away and then

107
00:04:09,409 --> 00:04:11,929
I sneak up quick and wait behind a tree."

108
00:04:11,949 --> 00:04:13,340
When the pilot walked past,

109
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:15,907
Ulungura followed him, grabbed his wrist

110
00:04:15,927 --> 00:04:18,158
near the gun, and took his revolver.

111
00:04:18,178 --> 00:04:20,087
Then, he said, he walked backwards,

112
00:04:20,107 --> 00:04:22,016
pointing the pistol, and told him:

113
00:04:22,036 --> 00:04:23,000
"Stick 'em up."

114
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:27,548
Ulungura kept watch over his prisoner all

115
00:04:27,568 --> 00:04:30,705
night, and then handed him over to an

116
00:04:30,725 --> 00:04:33,073
Australian army sergeant named Leslie Powell.

117
00:04:33,093 --> 00:04:34,257
Powell was unarmed.

118
00:04:34,277 --> 00:04:36,624
So, the Australian War Memorial records,

119
00:04:36,644 --> 00:04:39,387
he marched the pilot into captivity with

120
00:04:39,407 --> 00:04:41,380
the pilot's own service pistol.

121
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:44,990
The Zero lay on Melville Island.

122
00:04:45,010 --> 00:04:48,137
It was the first intact Zero captured by

123
00:04:48,157 --> 00:04:48,923
the Allies.

124
00:04:48,943 --> 00:04:51,677
And its pilot was the first Japanese

125
00:04:51,697 --> 00:04:54,450
prisoner of war taken on Australian soil.

126
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:57,845
When the Australians questioned him,

127
00:04:57,865 --> 00:05:00,508
the pilot did not give his real name.

128
00:05:00,528 --> 00:05:04,380
He said he was Tadao Minami.

129
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:05,860
Even his story was invented:

130
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:07,932
the records the Memorial holds give two

131
00:05:07,952 --> 00:05:10,300
different versions of who he claimed to be

132
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:11,800
and what he had flown.

133
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:15,508
He was not the only one.

134
00:05:15,528 --> 00:05:17,490
A Japanese survivor of Cowra,

135
00:05:17,510 --> 00:05:20,045
Marekuni Takahara, told the Memorial in

136
00:05:20,065 --> 00:05:23,031
2004 that he had made up his false name in

137
00:05:23,051 --> 00:05:25,121
Darwin, and had told the Australians he

138
00:05:25,141 --> 00:05:27,212
was a steward on a fishing boat.

139
00:05:27,232 --> 00:05:29,601
"Many of us had false names," he said,

140
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:32,010
"and we just didn't talk about the past."

141
00:05:33,325 --> 00:05:35,782
A false name meant that no letter would

142
00:05:35,802 --> 00:05:38,259
reach his family, and no list would carry

143
00:05:38,279 --> 00:05:39,498
his name to Japan.

144
00:05:39,518 --> 00:05:41,665
As far as his village was concerned,

145
00:05:41,685 --> 00:05:44,685
Hajime Toyoshima could have died a hero

146
00:05:44,705 --> 00:05:45,505
over Darwin.

147
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:49,133
Under the name Minami, he went south.

148
00:05:49,153 --> 00:05:51,593
He was sent to a camp at Loveday,

149
00:05:51,612 --> 00:05:54,450
in South Australia, in March 1942,

150
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:57,180
then to Hay, in New South Wales,

151
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:57,960
that April.

152
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:01,406
On January ninth, 1943, he reached a camp

153
00:06:01,426 --> 00:06:02,570
outside the town of Cowra.

154
00:06:04,175 --> 00:06:07,078
Cowra was the centre of a farming district

155
00:06:07,098 --> 00:06:10,000
in the central west of New South Wales.

156
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:12,192
The camp stood three kilometres north-east

157
00:06:12,212 --> 00:06:13,288
of the town.

158
00:06:13,308 --> 00:06:17,495
It had been built in 1941 for Italian prisoners.

159
00:06:18,850 --> 00:06:21,035
Its official name was Number Twelve

160
00:06:21,055 --> 00:06:22,505
Prisoner of War Compound.

161
00:06:22,525 --> 00:06:25,446
From the air, it looked like a wheel.

162
00:06:25,466 --> 00:06:28,019
The whole compound was an octagon about

163
00:06:28,039 --> 00:06:29,489
eight hundred yards across,

164
00:06:29,509 --> 00:06:32,429
cut into four camps by two roads that

165
00:06:32,449 --> 00:06:33,899
crossed in the middle.

166
00:06:33,919 --> 00:06:36,105
Each camp was fenced with thick

167
00:06:36,125 --> 00:06:38,330
barbed-wire entanglements about eight feet high.

168
00:06:39,575 --> 00:06:41,884
The prisoners and guards called the long

169
00:06:41,904 --> 00:06:43,548
road through the middle Broadway.

170
00:06:43,568 --> 00:06:45,212
At night, six observation towers,

171
00:06:45,232 --> 00:06:47,542
each thirty feet high, swept the camps

172
00:06:47,562 --> 00:06:50,204
with their lights, and fixed lamps lit the

173
00:06:50,224 --> 00:06:51,555
wire and the roads.

174
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:56,188
Italians lived in one part of the compound.

175
00:06:56,208 --> 00:06:59,083
Camp B, one quarter of the wheel,

176
00:06:59,103 --> 00:07:01,150
held Japanese soldiers and sailors,

177
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:03,218
the corporals, sergeants and privates.

178
00:07:03,238 --> 00:07:06,112
Their officers were kept in a separate

179
00:07:06,132 --> 00:07:07,353
camp across Broadway.

180
00:07:07,373 --> 00:07:11,118
By August 1944, there were one thousand

181
00:07:11,138 --> 00:07:13,975
one hundred and four Japanese prisoners at Cowra.

182
00:07:15,075 --> 00:07:17,492
The camp was guarded by the twenty-second

183
00:07:17,512 --> 00:07:18,189
Garrison Battalion.

184
00:07:18,209 --> 00:07:20,626
Garrison battalions were for men who could

185
00:07:20,646 --> 00:07:22,716
not be sent to the front:

186
00:07:22,736 --> 00:07:25,849
men who were too old, or not fit enough.

187
00:07:25,869 --> 00:07:28,655
Many had fought in the First World War.

188
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:32,332
Charles Shepherd had been classed as fit

189
00:07:32,352 --> 00:07:34,784
only for base or garrison duty because,

190
00:07:34,804 --> 00:07:36,185
at his medical examination,

191
00:07:36,205 --> 00:07:39,688
he was found to be blind in his left eye.

192
00:07:39,708 --> 00:07:41,089
He was thirty-one, married,

193
00:07:41,109 --> 00:07:42,160
with three daughters.

194
00:07:43,525 --> 00:07:45,838
Ben Hardy was a motor mechanic from

195
00:07:45,858 --> 00:07:49,405
Willoughby, in Sydney, born in 1898.

196
00:07:49,425 --> 00:07:50,909
He was a fine shot.

197
00:07:50,929 --> 00:07:53,315
He had been champion of his local rifle

198
00:07:53,335 --> 00:07:56,022
club, and he was known as an authority on

199
00:07:56,042 --> 00:07:57,245
the Vickers machine gun.

200
00:07:58,575 --> 00:08:00,815
Ralph Jones had been born in Gorleston,

201
00:08:00,835 --> 00:08:03,275
on the English coast, in 1900,

202
00:08:03,295 --> 00:08:06,099
and had come to Australia in the 1920s.

203
00:08:06,119 --> 00:08:09,276
He was a labourer from the town of Crookwell.

204
00:08:09,296 --> 00:08:12,099
As a young soldier after the First World

205
00:08:12,119 --> 00:08:14,923
War, he had served with the army of

206
00:08:14,943 --> 00:08:16,355
occupation on the Rhine.

207
00:08:18,025 --> 00:08:20,420
A guard at Cowra, Sergeant Ashley Cooper,

208
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:22,490
later described how they were armed.

209
00:08:22,510 --> 00:08:24,215
They carried point three-oh-three rifles.

210
00:08:24,235 --> 00:08:27,320
They had been shown how to work a Bren

211
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:30,080
gun, a Sten gun and an Owen gun,

212
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:32,495
but, he said, "we had no practical

213
00:08:32,515 --> 00:08:34,585
experience on a rifle range whatsoever."

214
00:08:36,025 --> 00:08:38,752
On the edge of the Japanese compound stood

215
00:08:38,772 --> 00:08:39,782
two four-wheeled trailers,

216
00:08:39,802 --> 00:08:41,842
each carrying a Vickers machine gun.

217
00:08:41,862 --> 00:08:44,588
One of them, Cooper said, would become the

218
00:08:44,608 --> 00:08:46,325
gun of Hardy and Jones.

219
00:08:47,725 --> 00:08:50,470
Most of the guards did not believe the

220
00:08:50,490 --> 00:08:52,198
Japanese would ever attack them.

221
00:08:52,218 --> 00:08:54,618
"We'd never ever thought they'd even try

222
00:08:54,638 --> 00:08:55,654
it," Cooper said.

223
00:08:55,674 --> 00:08:58,419
"It was such a ridiculous thought on our

224
00:08:58,439 --> 00:08:59,239
part."

225
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:02,483
Life in Camp B was comfortable.

226
00:09:02,503 --> 00:09:04,878
A military court of inquiry later found

227
00:09:04,898 --> 00:09:07,615
that the Japanese were housed and fed on

228
00:09:07,635 --> 00:09:09,668
the same scale as Australian troops,

229
00:09:09,688 --> 00:09:12,406
and that they had made no complaint about

230
00:09:12,426 --> 00:09:13,226
their treatment.

231
00:09:14,425 --> 00:09:16,834
The official history says that the

232
00:09:16,854 --> 00:09:19,263
prisoners grew stronger on their rations,

233
00:09:19,283 --> 00:09:21,691
and on "constant wrestling and baseball".

234
00:09:21,711 --> 00:09:23,715
They made their own equipment.

235
00:09:23,735 --> 00:09:26,105
A survivor, Masayoshi Yamada,

236
00:09:26,125 --> 00:09:27,975
remembered that they took apart their

237
00:09:27,995 --> 00:09:29,865
leather boots to make baseball gloves.

238
00:09:31,175 --> 00:09:33,975
Another survivor, Teruo Murakami,

239
00:09:33,995 --> 00:09:36,255
later called camp life almost heavenly.

240
00:09:36,275 --> 00:09:38,790
Yamada said they lived very comfortably,

241
00:09:38,810 --> 00:09:41,015
with up to five cigarettes a day.

242
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:44,388
But comfort did not mean peace.

243
00:09:44,408 --> 00:09:46,496
A major of the garrison battalion,

244
00:09:46,516 --> 00:09:47,901
the novelist Edward Timms,

245
00:09:47,921 --> 00:09:50,360
wrote after the war that the prisoners

246
00:09:50,380 --> 00:09:52,468
read the Australians' humane treatment as

247
00:09:52,488 --> 00:09:53,873
a sign of weakness.

248
00:09:53,893 --> 00:09:56,332
In his words, they believed it "sprang

249
00:09:56,352 --> 00:09:58,460
from our secret fear of them."

250
00:10:00,075 --> 00:10:03,301
In June 1944, two months before the

251
00:10:03,321 --> 00:10:05,410
breakout, a Korean prisoner told the

252
00:10:05,430 --> 00:10:07,519
camp's intelligence officer that he had

253
00:10:07,539 --> 00:10:09,628
heard Japanese prisoners talking about a

254
00:10:09,648 --> 00:10:09,980
plan:

255
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,440
to attack the garrison, seize weapons and

256
00:10:12,460 --> 00:10:13,515
ammunition, and escape.

257
00:10:14,850 --> 00:10:16,828
According to a newspaper account published

258
00:10:16,848 --> 00:10:19,160
decades later, the report was passed to

259
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:21,492
the officer in charge of the Japanese

260
00:10:21,512 --> 00:10:23,510
compound, who dismissed it as nonsense.

261
00:10:25,025 --> 00:10:27,866
The official history says that early in

262
00:10:27,886 --> 00:10:29,909
August, the camp's headquarters received

263
00:10:29,929 --> 00:10:32,769
word that the Japanese were discussing a

264
00:10:32,789 --> 00:10:33,587
mass outbreak.

265
00:10:33,607 --> 00:10:35,221
Extra precautions were taken.

266
00:10:35,241 --> 00:10:38,082
And the Australians made a decision that

267
00:10:38,102 --> 00:10:40,145
would set everything in motion.

268
00:10:41,525 --> 00:10:42,740
The camp was overcrowded.

269
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:44,901
The army decided to move the Japanese

270
00:10:44,921 --> 00:10:47,371
privates out of Cowra, to the big prisoner

271
00:10:47,391 --> 00:10:50,459
of war camp at Hay, far out on the plains

272
00:10:50,479 --> 00:10:51,405
to the west.

273
00:10:53,175 --> 00:10:55,063
The Geneva Convention required that

274
00:10:55,083 --> 00:10:56,972
prisoners be given twenty-four hours'

275
00:10:56,992 --> 00:10:58,498
notice of a move.

276
00:10:58,518 --> 00:11:00,407
So on Friday, August fourth,

277
00:11:00,427 --> 00:11:03,460
the Japanese camp leader was told that all

278
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:06,132
the Japanese at Cowra, except the officers

279
00:11:06,152 --> 00:11:07,658
and the non-commissioned officers,

280
00:11:07,678 --> 00:11:10,350
would leave for Hay on the seventh.

281
00:11:11,050 --> 00:11:13,569
To the Australians it was a transfer.

282
00:11:13,589 --> 00:11:17,195
To the men in Camp B it was something else.

283
00:11:17,215 --> 00:11:19,734
In the Japanese forces, the bond between

284
00:11:19,754 --> 00:11:22,273
the sergeants and their privates was often

285
00:11:22,293 --> 00:11:24,811
compared to that between older and younger

286
00:11:24,831 --> 00:11:25,174
brothers.

287
00:11:25,194 --> 00:11:27,370
The move would split them apart.

288
00:11:28,650 --> 00:11:30,056
The Japanese leaders objected.

289
00:11:30,076 --> 00:11:33,621
One of the men who led the camp was a

290
00:11:33,641 --> 00:11:36,970
sergeant major named Akira Kanazawa.

291
00:11:36,990 --> 00:11:38,962
The Australian War Memorial records one

292
00:11:38,982 --> 00:11:40,622
order he gave that night:

293
00:11:40,642 --> 00:11:42,614
Australian soldiers were to be attacked,

294
00:11:42,634 --> 00:11:45,290
but no prisoner was to harm a civilian.

295
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:49,009
That night, the leaders met in secret,

296
00:11:49,029 --> 00:11:51,213
and the court of inquiry would later find

297
00:11:51,233 --> 00:11:53,418
that the attack followed a meeting held in

298
00:11:53,438 --> 00:11:54,540
the camp at midnight.

299
00:11:55,850 --> 00:11:58,575
Then the men were asked to vote.

300
00:11:58,595 --> 00:12:00,929
According to survivors, the prisoners were

301
00:12:00,949 --> 00:12:04,066
split into small groups, and each man was

302
00:12:04,086 --> 00:12:06,027
handed a scrap of paper.

303
00:12:06,047 --> 00:12:08,772
One account says it was toilet paper.

304
00:12:08,792 --> 00:12:10,341
A circle meant yes.

305
00:12:10,361 --> 00:12:11,930
A cross meant no.

306
00:12:13,175 --> 00:12:15,305
Teruo Murakami was twenty-three.

307
00:12:15,325 --> 00:12:17,314
Decades later he told the Memorial:

308
00:12:17,334 --> 00:12:20,328
"I said I voted yes, but to be honest,

309
00:12:20,348 --> 00:12:23,007
I didn't think it was a good idea.

310
00:12:23,027 --> 00:12:25,686
I wanted to vote no." Years after that,

311
00:12:25,706 --> 00:12:27,695
he told a reporter more simply:

312
00:12:27,715 --> 00:12:29,055
"I drew a circle."

313
00:12:30,300 --> 00:12:33,653
Masayoshi Yamada said he had felt that he

314
00:12:33,673 --> 00:12:35,148
wanted to cling to life.

315
00:12:35,168 --> 00:12:36,941
But once the decision was made,

316
00:12:36,961 --> 00:12:39,632
he said, "I felt, okay, I come along with

317
00:12:39,652 --> 00:12:41,425
you." A Japanese survivor interviewed for

318
00:12:41,445 --> 00:12:43,816
a documentary in 2021 said there was a

319
00:12:43,836 --> 00:12:46,507
mood in which they had to say yes when

320
00:12:46,527 --> 00:12:48,320
they actually wanted to say otherwise.

321
00:12:49,725 --> 00:12:50,705
The circles won.

322
00:12:50,725 --> 00:12:52,705
The breakout would begin that night,

323
00:12:52,725 --> 00:12:55,725
at two o'clock, at the sound of a bugle.

324
00:12:57,300 --> 00:13:00,468
The men had been preparing for a long time.

325
00:13:00,488 --> 00:13:02,593
According to the court of inquiry,

326
00:13:02,613 --> 00:13:04,364
they had readied extra clothing,

327
00:13:04,384 --> 00:13:07,198
gloves and padding to get through the wire.

328
00:13:07,218 --> 00:13:09,677
Some wore their homemade baseball mitts to

329
00:13:09,697 --> 00:13:10,760
protect their hands.

330
00:13:12,050 --> 00:13:14,390
The official history lists their weapons:

331
00:13:14,410 --> 00:13:16,750
knives, baseball bats, clubs studded with

332
00:13:16,770 --> 00:13:19,110
nails and hooks, wire stilettos and

333
00:13:19,130 --> 00:13:19,978
garrotting cords.

334
00:13:19,998 --> 00:13:22,692
The blankets were for the barbed wire.

335
00:13:22,712 --> 00:13:25,794
They would throw them over the fence and

336
00:13:25,814 --> 00:13:26,614
climb across.

337
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,893
Yamada carried a baseball bat.

338
00:13:29,913 --> 00:13:32,358
He said it was not to attack anyone.

339
00:13:32,378 --> 00:13:34,824
He and a friend had made a pact.

340
00:13:34,844 --> 00:13:37,597
If one of them was shot and close to

341
00:13:37,617 --> 00:13:40,063
death, the other would strike him on the

342
00:13:40,083 --> 00:13:42,240
head, so that he would die quickly.

343
00:13:43,550 --> 00:13:46,853
Marekuni Takahara was told that the signal

344
00:13:46,873 --> 00:13:48,216
would come at two.

345
00:13:48,236 --> 00:13:50,260
He was in the first group.

346
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:52,985
His task was to cross Broadway and reach

347
00:13:53,005 --> 00:13:55,730
the Japanese officers' camp on the other side.

348
00:13:57,025 --> 00:13:59,966
Murakami said he joined believing he would

349
00:13:59,986 --> 00:14:02,087
not see the end of it.

350
00:14:02,107 --> 00:14:05,621
Most of the men in Camp B expected to die

351
00:14:05,641 --> 00:14:06,328
before morning.

352
00:14:06,348 --> 00:14:08,803
That, a historian at the Australian War

353
00:14:08,823 --> 00:14:10,923
Memorial has said, was the point.

354
00:14:10,943 --> 00:14:13,044
They knew they would not succeed.

355
00:14:13,064 --> 00:14:15,185
It was the attempt that mattered.

356
00:14:16,925 --> 00:14:18,955
At about two in the morning,

357
00:14:18,975 --> 00:14:21,688
a Japanese prisoner ran to the camp gates

358
00:14:21,708 --> 00:14:24,421
and shouted what seemed to be a warning.

359
00:14:24,441 --> 00:14:26,471
One account describes him as weeping,

360
00:14:26,491 --> 00:14:27,837
and in great fear.

361
00:14:27,857 --> 00:14:29,887
The guard on Broadway, Private Alfred

362
00:14:29,907 --> 00:14:32,278
Rolls, fired two shots into the air,

363
00:14:32,298 --> 00:14:33,665
the signal for trouble.

364
00:14:34,975 --> 00:14:36,363
Then the bugle sounded.

365
00:14:36,383 --> 00:14:38,827
It was blown, according to the Australian

366
00:14:38,847 --> 00:14:41,291
War Memorial, by the prisoner the camp

367
00:14:41,311 --> 00:14:43,545
knew as Tadao Minami:

368
00:14:43,565 --> 00:14:46,055
Hajime Toyoshima, the pilot from Darwin.

369
00:14:47,375 --> 00:14:50,237
With a cry of Banzai, three great mobs of

370
00:14:50,257 --> 00:14:52,760
prisoners broke out of their huts and ran

371
00:14:52,780 --> 00:14:55,283
at the wire, one on the north side,

372
00:14:55,303 --> 00:14:58,121
one on the west and one on the south.

373
00:14:58,141 --> 00:14:59,383
Some crawled under it.

374
00:14:59,403 --> 00:15:00,960
Many, a Memorial historian said,

375
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:03,167
simply threw themselves onto the wire so

376
00:15:03,187 --> 00:15:05,395
that others could climb over their bodies.

377
00:15:06,750 --> 00:15:09,234
Behind them, men who stayed in the camp

378
00:15:09,254 --> 00:15:10,800
set fire to the huts.

379
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:12,678
Eighteen of the twenty sleeping huts

380
00:15:12,698 --> 00:15:13,950
burned to the ground.

381
00:15:15,675 --> 00:15:17,967
Sergeant Cooper woke to a terrific noise:

382
00:15:17,987 --> 00:15:19,618
machine guns going flat out,

383
00:15:19,638 --> 00:15:21,929
pistols going off, flares going up into

384
00:15:21,949 --> 00:15:22,590
the sky.

385
00:15:22,610 --> 00:15:24,902
"I thought, oh, the bastards have broke,"

386
00:15:24,922 --> 00:15:27,874
he said, "and with that I dived into my

387
00:15:27,894 --> 00:15:29,875
trousers and jacket, over my pyjamas."

388
00:15:31,225 --> 00:15:34,176
He could see the huts burning in the

389
00:15:34,196 --> 00:15:34,919
Japanese compound.

390
00:15:34,939 --> 00:15:37,889
It was, he said, like a big bonfire.

391
00:15:37,909 --> 00:15:39,003
The floodlights short-circuited.

392
00:15:39,023 --> 00:15:41,974
In the dark, he said, the prisoners used

393
00:15:41,994 --> 00:15:44,965
their own dead to walk over the wire.

394
00:15:46,250 --> 00:15:49,230
Most of the guards who were off duty had

395
00:15:49,250 --> 00:15:50,897
gone to bed fully clothed,

396
00:15:50,917 --> 00:15:53,563
with their rifles and fifty rounds beside them.

397
00:15:53,583 --> 00:15:56,250
Now they ran out to reinforce the sentries.

398
00:15:57,550 --> 00:16:00,139
Private Charles Shepherd came out of the

399
00:16:00,159 --> 00:16:02,748
guardroom and was stabbed in the chest.

400
00:16:02,768 --> 00:16:03,866
He died there.

401
00:16:03,886 --> 00:16:05,750
He was thirty-one years old.

402
00:16:07,350 --> 00:16:09,022
The strongest group of prisoners,

403
00:16:09,042 --> 00:16:11,392
about four hundred men, broke through the

404
00:16:11,412 --> 00:16:13,084
wire on the north-west side.

405
00:16:13,104 --> 00:16:16,150
That was where Hardy and Jones had their gun.

406
00:16:17,475 --> 00:16:20,417
The two men had pushed their way through

407
00:16:20,437 --> 00:16:22,269
the prisoners to reach it.

408
00:16:22,289 --> 00:16:23,009
Hardy fired.

409
00:16:23,029 --> 00:16:24,860
Jones fed the ammunition belt.

410
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:27,823
They kept the Vickers firing into the mass

411
00:16:27,843 --> 00:16:30,785
of men coming at them, until they were

412
00:16:30,805 --> 00:16:31,605
overwhelmed.

413
00:16:32,575 --> 00:16:34,507
Hardy was clubbed to death.

414
00:16:34,527 --> 00:16:35,678
Jones was stabbed.

415
00:16:35,698 --> 00:16:38,801
The prisoners took the gun and swung it

416
00:16:38,821 --> 00:16:41,534
round to fire on the Australians' huts.

417
00:16:41,554 --> 00:16:43,115
It would not fire.

418
00:16:44,425 --> 00:16:45,874
Accounts differ on why.

419
00:16:45,894 --> 00:16:48,811
The official history says only that it jammed.

420
00:16:48,831 --> 00:16:50,647
Harry Gordon, the Australian journalist

421
00:16:50,667 --> 00:16:52,850
who spent years on the breakout,

422
00:16:52,870 --> 00:16:54,685
thought the Japanese had probably

423
00:16:54,705 --> 00:16:56,888
forgotten to swing the ammunition belt

424
00:16:56,908 --> 00:16:58,357
round with the gun.

425
00:16:58,377 --> 00:17:00,560
But the guards believed something else.

426
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:01,294
Cooper said:

427
00:17:01,314 --> 00:17:04,599
"I think it was Hardy that took the bolt

428
00:17:04,619 --> 00:17:08,638
out of the gun", so that it could not be fired.

429
00:17:08,658 --> 00:17:10,841
The Australian War Memorial says that

430
00:17:10,861 --> 00:17:14,165
Hardy's last act was to make the gun unusable.

431
00:17:15,375 --> 00:17:17,185
At dawn, Cooper saw the gun.

432
00:17:17,205 --> 00:17:19,930
In front of it, he said, the dead were

433
00:17:19,950 --> 00:17:22,980
piled in a half circle, three to four feet high.

434
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,420
"Just like a pile of firewood," he said,

435
00:17:25,440 --> 00:17:26,355
"with dead bodies."

436
00:17:28,125 --> 00:17:29,650
According to the official history,

437
00:17:29,670 --> 00:17:31,814
not one Japanese prisoner got across the

438
00:17:31,834 --> 00:17:34,595
road that ran through the middle of the compound.

439
00:17:34,615 --> 00:17:37,376
The fire there was so heavy that more than

440
00:17:37,396 --> 00:17:39,849
two hundred men took shelter in a deep

441
00:17:39,869 --> 00:17:41,105
ditch at its side.

442
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:45,242
Takahara reached Broadway with the first group.

443
00:17:45,262 --> 00:17:48,018
By the time he got there, he said,

444
00:17:48,038 --> 00:17:50,100
there were already about thirty bodies

445
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:51,488
lying on the road.

446
00:17:51,508 --> 00:17:54,611
A bullet grazed him and hit the man behind

447
00:17:54,631 --> 00:17:55,999
him in the heart.

448
00:17:56,019 --> 00:17:57,060
He died instantly.

449
00:17:58,425 --> 00:18:02,107
Yamada and Murakami both ended up lying in

450
00:18:02,127 --> 00:18:03,297
the side ditch.

451
00:18:03,317 --> 00:18:06,469
They stayed there until the sun came up.

452
00:18:06,489 --> 00:18:07,921
Then, Murakami remembered,

453
00:18:07,941 --> 00:18:10,949
an Australian came and spoke to them in Japanese.

454
00:18:10,969 --> 00:18:13,304
"We will not harm you," he said,

455
00:18:13,324 --> 00:18:15,323
"but you have to come back.

456
00:18:15,343 --> 00:18:17,025
Get out of the ditch."

457
00:18:18,350 --> 00:18:20,356
The shooting went on, Cooper said,

458
00:18:20,376 --> 00:18:22,720
from two o'clock until about half past

459
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:23,733
five, until dawn.

460
00:18:23,753 --> 00:18:27,130
To him, it passed like the snap of a finger.

461
00:18:28,850 --> 00:18:31,232
When it was light, the Australians walked

462
00:18:31,252 --> 00:18:32,261
into Camp B.

463
00:18:32,281 --> 00:18:33,977
Almost every hut was ash.

464
00:18:33,997 --> 00:18:36,379
Inside the burned huts they found the

465
00:18:36,399 --> 00:18:39,830
bodies of twelve men who had died in the fire.

466
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:43,769
The court of inquiry counted how the

467
00:18:43,789 --> 00:18:44,921
prisoners had died.

468
00:18:44,941 --> 00:18:47,610
Twenty had died by hanging or strangling,

469
00:18:47,630 --> 00:18:51,066
done by their own hands or by each other.

470
00:18:51,086 --> 00:18:52,603
Nine had stabbed themselves.

471
00:18:52,623 --> 00:18:55,291
Two had lain down under a train.

472
00:18:55,311 --> 00:18:58,000
Most of the rest had been shot.

473
00:18:59,275 --> 00:19:01,257
Murakami remembered the fire.

474
00:19:01,277 --> 00:19:04,796
"We had a big fire and put blankets in to

475
00:19:04,816 --> 00:19:06,565
make it big," he said.

476
00:19:06,585 --> 00:19:08,355
"We wanted to burn everything."

477
00:19:09,700 --> 00:19:11,910
Just outside the wire, in a ditch,

478
00:19:11,930 --> 00:19:13,822
lay the body of the bugler.

479
00:19:13,842 --> 00:19:16,689
The record of his grave gives the cause of

480
00:19:16,709 --> 00:19:19,557
death as a gunshot wound to the chest and

481
00:19:19,577 --> 00:19:20,513
a self-inflicted wound.

482
00:19:20,533 --> 00:19:22,424
He was buried two days later,

483
00:19:22,444 --> 00:19:25,175
under the name Tadao Minami.

484
00:19:26,275 --> 00:19:28,780
Several hundred prisoners had got clear of

485
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:31,665
the camp, into the dark farmland around Cowra.

486
00:19:31,685 --> 00:19:34,189
Camp guards, soldiers from a training camp

487
00:19:34,209 --> 00:19:37,455
nearby and the local police went out after them.

488
00:19:38,675 --> 00:19:41,196
The official history says that in this

489
00:19:41,216 --> 00:19:43,374
phase the Japanese offered no resistance.

490
00:19:43,394 --> 00:19:45,914
A police constable from the village of

491
00:19:45,934 --> 00:19:48,475
Woodstock arrested eight of them by himself.

492
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:52,195
Many did not wait to be found.

493
00:19:52,215 --> 00:19:53,920
Some hanged themselves from trees.

494
00:19:53,940 --> 00:19:55,990
At about seven in the morning,

495
00:19:56,010 --> 00:19:58,750
Cooper said, two men lay down on the

496
00:19:58,770 --> 00:20:00,840
railway line between Blayney and Cowra.

497
00:20:02,450 --> 00:20:04,272
That evening, a young lieutenant named

498
00:20:04,292 --> 00:20:06,421
Harry Doncaster led a party of trainee

499
00:20:06,441 --> 00:20:09,184
soldiers to search the hills north of the town.

500
00:20:09,204 --> 00:20:12,254
Some of the men had been in the army only

501
00:20:12,274 --> 00:20:14,730
a few weeks, so they carried no rifles.

502
00:20:16,125 --> 00:20:19,169
At dusk they came upon a group of escaped

503
00:20:19,189 --> 00:20:19,510
prisoners.

504
00:20:19,530 --> 00:20:22,233
One of them stood on a large rock,

505
00:20:22,253 --> 00:20:23,255
holding a knife.

506
00:20:23,275 --> 00:20:25,638
With no better weapon, Doncaster picked up

507
00:20:25,658 --> 00:20:26,319
a rock.

508
00:20:26,339 --> 00:20:28,702
The trainees, by their own admission at

509
00:20:28,722 --> 00:20:31,105
the inquiry, lost their nerve and ran.

510
00:20:32,400 --> 00:20:33,624
Doncaster was killed.

511
00:20:33,644 --> 00:20:36,943
When he was found, the bodies of ten

512
00:20:36,963 --> 00:20:39,017
Japanese prisoners lay around him.

513
00:20:39,037 --> 00:20:41,940
They had died by their own hands.

514
00:20:43,550 --> 00:20:46,113
If you want more prisoner of war stories

515
00:20:46,133 --> 00:20:48,830
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

516
00:20:48,850 --> 00:20:49,203
Frontlines.

517
00:20:49,223 --> 00:20:52,186
And if you have a question about Cowra,

518
00:20:52,206 --> 00:20:54,051
leave it in the comments.

519
00:20:54,071 --> 00:20:55,190
We read them.

520
00:20:56,425 --> 00:20:58,920
The people of Cowra heard about the

521
00:20:58,940 --> 00:21:01,077
breakout at seven o'clock that Saturday

522
00:21:01,097 --> 00:21:03,233
morning, five hours after it began.

523
00:21:03,253 --> 00:21:05,389
Hundreds of escaped prisoners were loose

524
00:21:05,409 --> 00:21:07,565
in the farmland around the town.

525
00:21:08,850 --> 00:21:11,300
Whether because of Kanazawa's order or

526
00:21:11,320 --> 00:21:13,645
not, no civilian was hurt during the

527
00:21:13,665 --> 00:21:15,655
breakout, and the newspapers reported that

528
00:21:15,675 --> 00:21:17,350
no home was broken into.

529
00:21:19,025 --> 00:21:21,582
On a farm outside Cowra that day,

530
00:21:21,602 --> 00:21:23,423
May Weir was making scones.

531
00:21:23,443 --> 00:21:25,632
Her daughter Margaret later told the

532
00:21:25,652 --> 00:21:27,125
Memorial what happened next.

533
00:21:28,375 --> 00:21:30,775
The farm's mechanic, Stan Bradtke,

534
00:21:30,795 --> 00:21:33,385
came down the path with three Japanese

535
00:21:33,405 --> 00:21:35,622
prisoners in their maroon prison clothes.

536
00:21:35,642 --> 00:21:38,605
"What do they want, Stan?" Mrs Weir asked.

537
00:21:38,625 --> 00:21:41,235
"They want food, Mrs Weir," he said.

538
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:44,320
"Well, just take them round to the side

539
00:21:44,340 --> 00:21:47,086
verandah," she said, "and I'll be with you

540
00:21:47,106 --> 00:21:47,998
in a minute.

541
00:21:48,018 --> 00:21:49,840
I'll just finish making these scones."

542
00:21:51,250 --> 00:21:53,761
Margaret was sent to call the military.

543
00:21:53,781 --> 00:21:56,654
When she came back, her mother was pouring

544
00:21:56,674 --> 00:21:57,738
the prisoners tea.

545
00:21:57,758 --> 00:22:00,631
They were wearing old felt hats which the

546
00:22:00,651 --> 00:22:03,162
family later suspected they had found in

547
00:22:03,182 --> 00:22:04,246
the shearing shed.

548
00:22:04,266 --> 00:22:07,139
The three men ate for about ten minutes.

549
00:22:07,159 --> 00:22:09,690
Then they got up, and thanked her.

550
00:22:10,875 --> 00:22:13,702
One of those three men would come back to

551
00:22:13,722 --> 00:22:15,284
that farm, forty years later.

552
00:22:15,304 --> 00:22:17,835
And the family would still have the jug.

553
00:22:19,550 --> 00:22:21,976
The search went on for nine days.

554
00:22:21,996 --> 00:22:24,421
In the end, three hundred and thirty-four

555
00:22:24,441 --> 00:22:26,867
prisoners were brought back to the camp.

556
00:22:26,887 --> 00:22:28,963
Twenty-five of them came back dead,

557
00:22:28,983 --> 00:22:30,730
most by their own hands.

558
00:22:31,975 --> 00:22:33,570
The total was terrible.

559
00:22:33,590 --> 00:22:36,397
The court of inquiry counted two hundred

560
00:22:36,417 --> 00:22:38,820
and thirty-one Japanese dead and one

561
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:40,436
hundred and eight wounded.

562
00:22:40,456 --> 00:22:43,263
Three more later died of their wounds,

563
00:22:43,283 --> 00:22:46,090
which is why the official history gives

564
00:22:46,110 --> 00:22:47,725
two hundred and thirty-four.

565
00:22:48,425 --> 00:22:50,233
Four Australians had been killed:

566
00:22:50,253 --> 00:22:52,061
Hardy, Jones, Shepherd and Doncaster.

567
00:22:52,081 --> 00:22:54,621
A fifth, Sergeant Thomas Hancock of the

568
00:22:54,641 --> 00:22:57,180
Volunteer Defence Corps, was hit by a

569
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:59,740
comrade's rifle that went off by accident

570
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:02,685
during the search, and died nine days later.

571
00:23:03,900 --> 00:23:06,440
It was the largest prison break of the

572
00:23:06,460 --> 00:23:07,400
Second World War.

573
00:23:07,420 --> 00:23:10,600
And it was the only land battle of the war

574
00:23:10,620 --> 00:23:11,900
fought on Australian soil.

575
00:23:13,675 --> 00:23:15,969
A military court of inquiry sat from

576
00:23:15,989 --> 00:23:18,283
August seventh to fifteenth and heard more

577
00:23:18,303 --> 00:23:19,295
than sixty witnesses.

578
00:23:20,750 --> 00:23:22,935
On September eighth, Prime Minister John

579
00:23:22,955 --> 00:23:25,876
Curtin read its findings to the House of

580
00:23:25,896 --> 00:23:26,243
Representatives.

581
00:23:26,263 --> 00:23:28,449
He did not name the camp.

582
00:23:28,469 --> 00:23:31,022
He said that over nine hundred Japanese

583
00:23:31,042 --> 00:23:33,962
prisoners of war in a camp in Australia

584
00:23:33,982 --> 00:23:36,535
had made an unprovoked mass attack upon

585
00:23:36,555 --> 00:23:37,355
their guards.

586
00:23:38,625 --> 00:23:41,180
The court found that the prisoners had

587
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:43,388
been treated according to the Geneva

588
00:23:43,408 --> 00:23:45,596
Convention, and that the attack was

589
00:23:45,616 --> 00:23:48,171
characterized by "a suicidal disregard of life".

590
00:23:48,191 --> 00:23:50,379
Its conclusion was that the responsibility

591
00:23:50,399 --> 00:23:52,218
rested entirely on the prisoners

592
00:23:52,238 --> 00:23:54,794
themselves, and that it had been their

593
00:23:54,814 --> 00:23:57,001
intention to engage in suicidal combat

594
00:23:57,021 --> 00:23:58,125
with their guards.

595
00:23:59,875 --> 00:24:02,153
The government kept the details quiet.

596
00:24:02,173 --> 00:24:04,834
Newspapers were not allowed to print the

597
00:24:04,854 --> 00:24:05,983
number of dead.

598
00:24:06,003 --> 00:24:08,664
In Australia the event became known simply

599
00:24:08,684 --> 00:24:10,195
as the Cowra incident.

600
00:24:10,215 --> 00:24:12,493
When Sydney's Daily Telegraph printed more

601
00:24:12,513 --> 00:24:15,174
than the censors allowed, a file was

602
00:24:15,194 --> 00:24:17,875
opened on their breach of the rules.

603
00:24:19,225 --> 00:24:21,406
The reason was the twenty-one thousand

604
00:24:21,426 --> 00:24:22,874
Australians in Japanese camps.

605
00:24:22,894 --> 00:24:25,442
The authorities feared that if the story

606
00:24:25,462 --> 00:24:27,644
reached Japan in the wrong form,

607
00:24:27,664 --> 00:24:29,845
Australian prisoners would pay for it.

608
00:24:29,865 --> 00:24:32,781
Cooper said that he once stopped a soldier

609
00:24:32,801 --> 00:24:35,716
who wanted to shoot a group of captured

610
00:24:35,736 --> 00:24:37,917
Japanese, by reminding him of the

611
00:24:37,937 --> 00:24:39,405
Australians in Japanese hands.

612
00:24:40,775 --> 00:24:43,511
The Australian government sent its report

613
00:24:43,531 --> 00:24:45,348
through Switzerland, the neutral

614
00:24:45,368 --> 00:24:48,104
protecting power, to the Japanese government.

615
00:24:48,124 --> 00:24:50,859
Japan's own archives hold the file.

616
00:24:50,879 --> 00:24:52,237
Its heading reads:

617
00:24:52,257 --> 00:24:53,635
protest to Australia.

618
00:24:55,450 --> 00:24:58,417
The secrecy lasted long after the war.

619
00:24:58,437 --> 00:25:00,977
It was not until September first,

620
00:25:00,997 --> 00:25:03,532
1950, that the London Gazette announced

621
00:25:03,552 --> 00:25:05,860
two posthumous awards of the George Cross,

622
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:07,855
Britain's highest award for gallantry not

623
00:25:07,875 --> 00:25:09,870
in the face of the enemy.

624
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:13,708
The citation reads that Hardy and Jones

625
00:25:13,728 --> 00:25:16,235
"stood their ground and continued to work

626
00:25:16,255 --> 00:25:18,402
the gun until bashed to death,

627
00:25:18,422 --> 00:25:19,846
displaying outstanding gallantry and

628
00:25:19,866 --> 00:25:22,735
devotion to duty in their fight against an

629
00:25:22,755 --> 00:25:24,179
overwhelming onslaught of fanatical

630
00:25:24,199 --> 00:25:24,999
Japanese."

631
00:25:26,075 --> 00:25:28,822
Ben Hardy's widowed mother died just days

632
00:25:28,842 --> 00:25:30,798
before the award was announced.

633
00:25:30,818 --> 00:25:33,565
His sister Beatrice received the cross in

634
00:25:33,585 --> 00:25:36,535
Sydney in 1952.

635
00:25:36,555 --> 00:25:38,480
Ralph Jones's brother Walter received his

636
00:25:38,500 --> 00:25:40,101
from King George the Sixth,

637
00:25:40,121 --> 00:25:42,715
at Buckingham Palace, on behalf of their mother.

638
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,540
Because Cowra was not counted as a war

639
00:25:46,560 --> 00:25:49,100
zone, his widow, Linda, was refused a war

640
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:49,740
widow's pension.

641
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:51,660
She could not support their three

642
00:25:51,680 --> 00:25:53,900
daughters, and the girls were made wards

643
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:54,880
of the state.

644
00:25:56,575 --> 00:25:58,973
The surviving prisoners were split up and

645
00:25:58,993 --> 00:26:02,082
sent to the camps at Hay and at Murchison,

646
00:26:02,102 --> 00:26:02,773
in Victoria.

647
00:26:02,793 --> 00:26:05,695
In February and March 1946,

648
00:26:05,715 --> 00:26:07,775
they were shipped home to a defeated Japan.

649
00:26:09,125 --> 00:26:12,061
They did not go home as heroes.

650
00:26:12,081 --> 00:26:15,018
Many went home under their false names.

651
00:26:15,038 --> 00:26:18,205
A Japanese filmmaker, Yasuhiro Mitsuda,

652
00:26:18,225 --> 00:26:19,194
who interviewed survivors,

653
00:26:19,214 --> 00:26:21,500
said the men believed they would have

654
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:23,807
become heroes by dying, but were only

655
00:26:23,827 --> 00:26:25,785
ordinary men because they had survived.

656
00:26:25,805 --> 00:26:28,091
According to a New South Wales museum,

657
00:26:28,111 --> 00:26:30,398
half of them never told their families

658
00:26:30,418 --> 00:26:32,725
that they had been prisoners at all.

659
00:26:33,975 --> 00:26:36,715
One survivor, Seiichiro Tachibana,

660
00:26:36,735 --> 00:26:37,963
had Hansen's disease, leprosy,

661
00:26:37,983 --> 00:26:41,709
and had been kept in a tent of his own at Cowra.

662
00:26:41,729 --> 00:26:43,582
He knew nothing of the plan.

663
00:26:43,602 --> 00:26:47,035
He kept his false name for the rest of his life.

664
00:26:48,325 --> 00:26:50,505
Takahara built a new life.

665
00:26:50,525 --> 00:26:53,700
Later he would joke that he was a graduate

666
00:26:53,720 --> 00:26:54,785
of Cowra University.

667
00:26:56,550 --> 00:26:58,330
The dead were buried at Cowra in August

668
00:26:58,350 --> 00:27:01,901
1944, next to the cemetery where the four

669
00:27:01,921 --> 00:27:02,721
Australians lay.

670
00:27:03,875 --> 00:27:06,159
One of the Japanese graves was marked with

671
00:27:06,179 --> 00:27:09,135
the name Tadao Minami.

672
00:27:09,155 --> 00:27:11,515
In the 1980s, Japanese and Australian

673
00:27:11,535 --> 00:27:13,895
researchers established who he really was:

674
00:27:13,915 --> 00:27:17,355
Hajime Toyoshima, of the carrier Hiryu,

675
00:27:17,375 --> 00:27:19,395
the first Japanese prisoner taken on

676
00:27:19,415 --> 00:27:20,215
Australian soil.

677
00:27:21,425 --> 00:27:22,513
His bugle survived.

678
00:27:22,533 --> 00:27:25,467
It had been made in London by the

679
00:27:25,487 --> 00:27:27,387
instrument makers Boosey and Hawkes,

680
00:27:27,407 --> 00:27:28,441
in the 1930s.

681
00:27:28,461 --> 00:27:31,251
How it reached a Japanese prisoner camp in

682
00:27:31,271 --> 00:27:33,709
New South Wales is still a mystery.

683
00:27:33,729 --> 00:27:36,870
Major Timms took it home and hung it in

684
00:27:36,890 --> 00:27:38,626
his sitting room in Sydney.

685
00:27:38,646 --> 00:27:41,787
After his death, his widow gave it to the

686
00:27:41,807 --> 00:27:44,265
Australian War Memorial, where it is today.

687
00:27:45,950 --> 00:27:49,564
In 1952, the local branch of the Returned

688
00:27:49,584 --> 00:27:51,617
Services League, the old soldiers of

689
00:27:51,637 --> 00:27:54,375
Cowra, began to look after the Japanese graves.

690
00:27:55,075 --> 00:27:58,654
In 1964, on the twentieth anniversary of

691
00:27:58,674 --> 00:28:00,532
the breakout, the Japanese ambassador

692
00:28:00,552 --> 00:28:02,786
opened the Cowra Japanese War Cemetery.

693
00:28:02,806 --> 00:28:06,168
It is the only Japanese war cemetery in Australia.

694
00:28:06,188 --> 00:28:08,422
The Japanese who died anywhere in

695
00:28:08,442 --> 00:28:10,676
Australia during the war were brought

696
00:28:10,696 --> 00:28:12,931
there, five hundred and twenty-four in

697
00:28:12,951 --> 00:28:15,936
all, including the men of Cowra and the

698
00:28:15,956 --> 00:28:17,835
airmen shot down over Darwin.

699
00:28:19,200 --> 00:28:23,840
In 1979, a Japanese garden opened in Cowra.

700
00:28:23,860 --> 00:28:26,360
Its designer, Ken Nakajima,

701
00:28:26,380 --> 00:28:28,509
deliberately left a grove of gum trees

702
00:28:28,529 --> 00:28:30,657
standing at the top of the hill.

703
00:28:30,677 --> 00:28:32,806
They stand for the Australian soldiers who

704
00:28:32,826 --> 00:28:33,626
were killed.

705
00:28:35,175 --> 00:28:38,076
In 1984, on the fortieth anniversary,

706
00:28:38,096 --> 00:28:40,517
survivors of the breakout came back to Cowra.

707
00:28:40,537 --> 00:28:43,569
One of them was one of the three men who

708
00:28:43,589 --> 00:28:45,115
had eaten May Weir's scones.

709
00:28:46,475 --> 00:28:48,383
He went back to the farm.

710
00:28:48,403 --> 00:28:51,275
Her son Bruce said he ran around the place

711
00:28:51,295 --> 00:28:53,524
like a little dog off a chain.

712
00:28:53,544 --> 00:28:56,095
Margaret told him the family still had the

713
00:28:56,115 --> 00:28:58,987
jug her mother had used to pour him tea.

714
00:28:59,007 --> 00:29:00,935
It was the very same jug.

715
00:29:02,250 --> 00:29:04,610
Takahara came back too, and persuaded

716
00:29:04,630 --> 00:29:06,850
twelve Buddhist priests to come with him.

717
00:29:06,870 --> 00:29:10,067
Yamada became president of the Cowra

718
00:29:10,087 --> 00:29:11,218
association in Japan.

719
00:29:11,238 --> 00:29:13,902
"Everybody thought we would die," he said,

720
00:29:13,922 --> 00:29:16,587
"but something happened, and we are still

721
00:29:16,607 --> 00:29:17,407
alive."

722
00:29:18,700 --> 00:29:22,861
Teruo Murakami, the man who drew a circle,

723
00:29:22,881 --> 00:29:25,983
made his last trip to Cowra in August

724
00:29:26,003 --> 00:29:27,934
2019, for the seventy-fifth anniversary.

725
00:29:27,954 --> 00:29:29,125
He was ninety-eight.

726
00:29:29,825 --> 00:29:32,910
He said he had voted yes because he wanted

727
00:29:32,930 --> 00:29:33,600
to die.

728
00:29:33,620 --> 00:29:34,635
And he said:

729
00:29:34,655 --> 00:29:37,740
"I don't want any young person to do the

730
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:40,500
things we have done, because it's such a

731
00:29:40,520 --> 00:29:41,880
miserable thing." His message,

732
00:29:41,900 --> 00:29:43,260
he said, was simple.

733
00:29:43,280 --> 00:29:43,950
"No war.

734
00:29:43,970 --> 00:29:45,005
Never, never again."

735
00:29:46,375 --> 00:29:48,863
He died in Japan in September 2023,

736
00:29:48,883 --> 00:29:51,730
at the age of one hundred and three.

737
00:29:51,750 --> 00:29:54,975
He was the last survivor of the Cowra breakout.

738
00:29:56,675 --> 00:29:58,610
If your father, grandfather or uncle

739
00:29:58,630 --> 00:30:00,239
served with the twenty-second Garrison

740
00:30:00,259 --> 00:30:02,195
Battalion, guarded the camps at Cowra,

741
00:30:02,215 --> 00:30:04,801
Hay or Murchison, or was a prisoner there,

742
00:30:04,821 --> 00:30:07,082
tell us his name in the comments.

743
00:30:07,102 --> 00:30:10,035
We would like to know what he told you.

744
00:30:11,625 --> 00:30:13,957
Today, almost nothing is left of Number

745
00:30:13,977 --> 00:30:15,637
Twelve Prisoner of War Compound.

746
00:30:15,657 --> 00:30:18,325
After the war the huts were taken away,

747
00:30:18,345 --> 00:30:21,705
and the barbed wire was sold off to local farmers.

748
00:30:23,075 --> 00:30:25,048
On the night of the eightieth anniversary,

749
00:30:25,068 --> 00:30:27,041
in August 2024, people gathered at the

750
00:30:27,061 --> 00:30:29,888
site of the camp at half past one in the

751
00:30:29,908 --> 00:30:32,166
morning, the hour the breakout is said to

752
00:30:32,186 --> 00:30:32,986
have begun.

753
00:30:34,075 --> 00:30:36,397
Three kilometres away, in the Japanese War

754
00:30:36,417 --> 00:30:38,404
Cemetery, the cemetery's records list a

755
00:30:38,424 --> 00:30:41,080
grave under the false name he chose in

756
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:43,935
Darwin, Tadao Minami.

757
00:30:43,955 --> 00:30:45,830
Beside it, they give the real one,

758
00:30:45,850 --> 00:30:47,455
which took forty years to find.

759
00:30:47,475 --> 00:30:51,495
Hajime Toyoshima, the pilot who blew the bugle.

